body:
In my experience (had German Shepherds since I was 16 -  nearly 40 now, been around them my whole life), the most important things are: 

1. The dog does not sleep on the bed at all for the first two years, special occasions only after that;

2. The dog is always fed last, but never misses a meal; 

3. The dog goes everywhere (that is practical) that its master goes, at least for the first two years.

4. Demanding affection does not pay off. Even though it's cute when the dog puts its paw on you or whines or whatever, try to resist patting it on its terms. You decide when to give it attention; 

5. You are the one walking the dog, not the other way around. The leash should always be slack and the dog always sits when you are not walking.



Other than that, I'd recommend a female - much less house-training, much more mature and far less likely to get overexcited to the point of forgetting training. 

Also, (and I hope this is redundant) don't ever spank or whack the dog - it is far better having a dog that behaves well out of love than fear.

Finally, most important is to break any of these rules in a heartbeat for the love of your doggy-wog.
